.TH MATH_X87 7 "May 2026" "Substrate" "Miscellaneous Information Manual"
.SH NAME
math_x87 \- i386/x87 inline-assembly optimisations in substrate libm
.SH SYNOPSIS
Most substrate
.BR libm (3)
entry points compile down to one or two x87 instructions on i386.
This page enumerates which functions take the hardware path, what
each path does, and what accuracy guarantees it provides.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.SS Hardware-backed functions
The following entry points dispatch directly to a single x87
instruction (or a tight loop around one).  All live in
.IR lib/m/src/ .
.PP
.TP 16
.B Function
.B x87 instruction (file)
.TP
.BR sin (), cos ()
.B fsin / fcos
+ fprem1 reduction
.RI ( math_trig.c )
.TP
.BR sincos ()
.B fsincos
+ fprem1 reduction
.RI ( math_trig.c )
.TP
.BR tan ()
.B fptan
+ fprem1 reduction
.RI ( math_trig.c )
.TP
.BR atan2 ()
.B fpatan
.RI ( math_trig.c )
.TP
.BR log (), log2 (), log10 ()
.B fyl2x
with appropriate base constant
.RI ( math_exp.c )
.TP
.BR log1p (), log2p1 (), log10p1 ()
.B fyl2xp1
for
.RB | x |
< 1\(en\(*r2/2; falls back otherwise
.RI ( math_exp.c )
.TP
.BR exp (), exp2 (), exp10 (), pow ()
.B frndint / f2xm1 / fscale
range-split
.RI ( math_exp.c ,
.IR x87_exp2 )
.TP
.BR exp2m1 ()
.B f2xm1
direct for
.RB | x |
\(<= 1
.RI ( math_exp.c )
.TP
.BR sqrt ()
.B fsqrt
.RI ( math_exp.c )
.TP
.BR rsqrt ()
.B fsqrt
then divide
.RI ( math_exp.c )
.TP
.BR fmod ()
.B fprem
in a C2-status-loop
.RI ( math_round.c )
.TP
.BR remainder ()
.B fprem1
in a C2-status-loop
.RI ( math_round.c )
.TP
.BR rint (), nearbyint ()
.B frndint
\(em
.BR nearbyint ()
additionally snapshots / restores
.B FE_INEXACT
so its required POSIX behaviour is preserved
.RI ( math_round.c )
.TP
.BR scalbn ()
.B fildl
then
.B fscale
.RI ( math_round.c )
.TP
.BR lrint (), llrint ()
.B fistp
in current rounding mode
.RI ( math_round.c )
.SS Range reduction
.B fsin
/
.B fcos
/
.B fptan
/
.B fsincos
have a hardware domain of |x| < 2\(ha63.  For larger arguments the
implementation enters a loop that uses
.B fprem1
against
.B 2\(*p
to reduce the argument into the native domain, then retries the
trig instruction.  Termination is detected via the FPU status word's
C2 bit \(em while C2=1, fprem1 has produced only a partial result and
must be repeated.
.PP
For
.B fprem
(used by
.BR fmod ())
and
.B fprem1
(used by
.BR remainder ()),
the same C2-loop pattern reduces oversized dividends down to a single
multiple of the divisor.  Without the loop the result for a dividend
above ~2\(ha32 \(mu divisor would be undefined.
.SS Accuracy
.TP
.BR sqrt (),
.BR fabs ()
\(em
correctly rounded (1 ULP).  These are IEEE-754 primitives.
.TP
.BR rint (),
.BR nearbyint (),
.BR scalbn ()
\(em
exact when the result is representable.
.TP
Trigonometric and logarithmic operations
\(em
faithfully rounded; on substrate this means typically 1\(en2 ULP from
the correctly-rounded result for ordinary arguments, climbing to 4\(en8
ULP for arguments near a singularity (e.g.
.BR sin "(\(*p) ≈"
0 with the residual coming entirely from \(*p reduction error).
.TP
.BR pow ()
\(em
faithfully rounded.  Internally a single
.B fyl2x
followed by the
.B frndint / f2xm1 / fscale
exp2 path; one rounding per multiplicative step, so error budgets
add but stay below ~3 ULP for non-pathological arguments.
.SS Fallback path
For non-i386 targets (currently stubbed but allowed for in the
source via
.BR __i386__ / __x86_64__
guards) every primitive has a portable C implementation:
range-reduction-by-argument, Taylor or minimax polynomial,
software exp/log.  Substrate's i386 build never enters those paths.
.SH NOTES
.SS GAS quirk: fsub / fsubr operand order
Some two-register forms of
.B fsub
and
.B fdiv
have their semantics swapped by GAS \(em
.B fsub %st(0), %st(1)
is assembled as the REVERSE-subtract opcode (
.RB DC " E0+i = FSUBR )"
rather than the FSUB you'd expect.  Substrate's exp / exp2 / pow paths
sidestep this by using the
.B fsub %st(i), %st(0)
form (D8 E0+i, unambiguous) for argument reduction.
.SS fenv_t struct layout
The 32-bit protected-mode FPU environment image written by
.B fnstenv
places each 16-bit register in the LOW HALF of a 32-bit slot, with
the upper half reserved.  Substrate's
.B fenv_t
is laid out with 32-bit fields wrapping each register so the struct's
field offsets align with the
.B fnstenv
output.  An earlier packed-16-bit layout put
.B __status_word
at offset 2 (the reserved upper half of the control-word slot) which
silently broke
.BR feraiseexcept (3),
.BR fesetexceptflag (3),
and
.BR nearbyint (3).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR exp (3),
.BR log (3),
.BR pow (3),
.BR sin (3),
.BR fenv (7),
.BR libm (3)
.PP
Intel\(rg 64 and IA-32 Architectures Software Developer's Manual,
Volume 1, Chapter 8 ("Programming with the x87 FPU"), and
Volume 2A entries for FSIN, FCOS, FSINCOS, FPTAN, FPATAN, FYL2X,
F2XM1, FSCALE, FPREM, FPREM1, FSQRT, FRNDINT, FISTP, FNSTENV /
